#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# logistic_sigmoid

#% text
# `logistic_sigmoid(z)` gives the logistic function `1/(1 + exp(-z))`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The function rises from 0 to 1 with value 1/2 at 0; `logistic_sigmoid(-z)` is `1 - logistic_sigmoid(z)`.

#% item
# `logistic_sigmoid(0)` is 1/2; other exact arguments stay unevaluated. Machine numbers and balls give numerical values, without overflow for large `abs(z)`.

#% item
# `logistic_sigmoid` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
logistic_sigmoid(0)
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
logistic_sigmoid(2.0)
#% output
#= 0.8807970779778823

#% cell
logistic_sigmoid(x)
#% output
#= logistic_sigmoid(x)

#% cell
logistic_sigmoid([-1.0, 0.0, 1.0])
#% output
#= [0.2689414213699951, 0.5, 0.7310585786300049]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Large arguments:

#% cell
logistic_sigmoid(800.0)
#% output
#= 1.0

#% cell
logistic_sigmoid(-800.0)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative is `s*(1 - s)`, checked by a difference quotient:

#% cell
s = logistic_sigmoid; (s(0.3 + 1.0e-6) - s(0.3 - 1.0e-6))/2.0e-6 - s(0.3)*(1 - s(0.3))
#% output
#= 1.3752637917363586e-11

#% text
# The relation to `tanh`:

#% cell
logistic_sigmoid(1.4) - (1 + tanh(0.7))/2
#% output
#= 0.0


#% text
# The derivative and the series:

#% cell
[d(logistic_sigmoid(x), x), series(logistic_sigmoid(x), x, 0, 5)]
#% output
#= [(1 - logistic_sigmoid(x))*logistic_sigmoid(x), 1/2 + x/4 - x^3/48 + x^5/480 + O(x^6)]

#% text
# At multiples of i pi:

#% cell
[logistic_sigmoid(2*im*pi), logistic_sigmoid(im*pi/2)]
#% output
#= [1/2, 1/2 + im/2]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `exp`, `tanh`, `log`
